---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { SITE, SOCIAL } from '../consts';
---

<!-- `profile` marks this URL as the page that is *about* the person, rather than
     one that merely carries his details — that is what search engines look for
     when deciding which single URL represents an entity. -->
<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="Yudha Bhakti Nugraha — engineer in Jakarta, Indonesia. Embedded systems, cloud platforms, applied AI, and the seam between them."
  profile
  works
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: 'Home', item: SITE.url }, { name: 'About' }]}
>
  <section class="hero">
    <h1>About</h1>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        I&rsquo;m Yudha Bhakti Nugraha &mdash; most people just call me Yudha &mdash; an
        engineer in Jakarta. I build and lead teams that deliver
        enterprise platforms &mdash; the systems finance, operations, and compliance
        functions actually run on &mdash; and I came to that from an unusual direction.
      </p>

      <p>
        My degree at Universitas Gadjah Mada was Computer Science <em>and</em> Electronics.
        That joint background is why hardware and edge computing keep reappearing in my
        work: a university nanosatellite, a radiation portal monitor for the national
        nuclear energy agency, a humanoid robot that placed third nationally, and later
        vehicle classification models running on Jetson devices at toll gates.
      </p>

      <p>
        Since then the work has moved up the stack &mdash; industrial IoT at a Telkom
        venture, then toll infrastructure, then agribusiness, where I led a product team
        across enterprise integration, platform engineering, and applied AI. The
        technologies changed. The instinct did not: assume the network is bad, assume the
        input is malformed, and find out whether anyone actually uses the thing.
      </p>

      <h2>What I&rsquo;m interested in</h2>

      <p>
        Four areas, roughly. <strong>Cloud architecture</strong> &mdash; designing and migrating
        systems across AWS, Azure, and GCP, with the infrastructure described in code rather
        than in a runbook. <strong>Enterprise integration</strong> &mdash; connecting ERP,
        HRIS, and banking systems through API middleware, and the stakeholder work that makes
        those programs land. <strong>Applied AI</strong> &mdash; computer vision for
        industrial inspection, and retrieval systems that answer questions spanning both
        structured records and unstructured documents. <strong>Platform engineering</strong>
        &mdash; CI/CD, source control at organisational scale, identity, and observability.
      </p>

      <p>
        A little of that is public. An open vehicle object-detection dataset I published was
        later selected into
        <a href="https://universe.roboflow.com/roboflow-100/vehicles-q0x2v">RF100</a>, an
        Intel-sponsored benchmark for how well detection models hold up outside the domain
        they were trained on.
      </p>

      <p>
        Outside of work I&rsquo;m building <a href="/projects">bykami</a>, a platform for a
        small multi-vertical business in Banyuwangi. It is the first thing in years where I
        own every architectural decision, and it has been a useful mirror.
      </p>

      <p>
        I also mentor. I spent 170 hours on Samsung Innovation Campus working with people
        entering the field, and I&rsquo;d do it again &mdash; teaching someone to debug their
        own reasoning is a much better use of an afternoon than debugging it for them.
      </p>

      <h2>Working with me</h2>

      <p>
        I value disciplined execution, clear writing, and technology that removes work rather
        than adding it. I&rsquo;d rather ship the boring version and learn from it than
        design the interesting one in a document.
      </p>

      <p>
        Reach me at <a href={`mailto:${SITE.email}`}>{SITE.email}</a>. I read everything, and
        I reply to most things.
      </p>
    </div>

    <ul class="inline-links">
      {SOCIAL.map((s) => <li><a href={s.href} rel="me noopener">{s.label}</a></li>)}
    </ul>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
